        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- It reuses the existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper rather than inlining RFC7807 construction in each handler, so no duplication is introduced \u2014 the shared title/type/timestamp/schemaValidationErrors fields stay in one place.\n- The new handlers now match the pattern already established by `ValidationExceptionHandler` and `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler` (same `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>>` signature, same `ResponseEntity.status(...).body(detail)` shape), so a maintainer would find them natural.\n- The `errors` extension member (previous `{\"errors\": [...]}` body) is preserved via `detail.setProperty(\"errors\", ...)`, consistent with how the validation handler attaches `schemaValidationErrors` \u2014 a coherent, additive way to keep the extra data.\n- Logic stays in the right unit: one handler per exception type, no handler is becoming a catch-all, and the per-field handlers remain distinct as the existing convention dictates.\n- Dead `java.util.Map` imports were removed and the Javadoc updated to match, so nothing is left stale.\n\nThe mild repetition across the four single-field handlers (each hardcoding its one field name) predates this change and follows the codebase's one-handler-per-exception design, so it isn't a regression."
